<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Abemama</h1><p class="st-meta">1840 CE – 1892 CE (approx.) · capital Abemama</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Gilbertese kingdom whose rulers conquered neighbouring atolls and controlled the copra trade, famed through Robert Louis Stevenson's portrait of Tem Binoka.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The chiefs of Abemama in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a> built up their power in the mid-19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tem-baiteke">Tem Baiteke</a>, using firearms obtained from traders, brought the nearby atolls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aranuka">Aranuka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuria-kiribati">Kuria</a> under his rule, creating a small island kingdom unlike the council-based societies of the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilberts</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tem-binoka">Tem Binoka</a>, who succeeded in the 1870s, Abemama was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a>: the king controlled land, labour and trade, and he dealt directly with European ships, selling copra and buying goods for himself. Foreign residents were strictly limited. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-louis-stevenson">Robert Louis Stevenson</a>, who stayed on the island in 1889, described Binoka in 'In the South Seas' as a shrewd and formidable ruler. Little else is documented in detail.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tem-binoka">Binoka</a> died in 1891, and in 1892 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a>, absorbing Abemama into what became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gilbert-and-ellice-islands">Gilbert and Ellice Islands</a>. Captain Davis of HMS Royalist proclaimed the protectorate there. The atoll is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiribati">Kiribati</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-abemama.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Abemama" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: Mvuijlst · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Abemama_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1840 CE – 1892 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Abemama</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tem Baiteke, Tem Binoka</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Gilbertese religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gilbertese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Kiribati</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Abemama</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1866&amp;focus=kingdom-of-abemama" aria-label="Kingdom of Abemama on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9510 2163 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Abemama lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9830 2419L9806 2386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9806 2386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9830 2419h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9830 2419h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Abemama. <a href="/atlas?year=1866&amp;focus=kingdom-of-abemama">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
